<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/8th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syro-Ephraimite War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 735–732 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Syro-Ephraimite War was a conflict of about 735–732 BCE in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rezin">Rezin</a> of Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekah">Pekah of Israel</a>, or Ephraim, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> after its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaz">Ahaz</a> refused to join their alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. Ahaz appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a>, who took Damascus, annexed much of Israel's territory and made Judah an Assyrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. The crisis forms the background to prophecies in the Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war set the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> against an alliance of Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>, based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, in the 8th century BCE. One theory holds that the allies' only aim was to force Judah to join their anti-Assyrian coalition. To achieve it, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rezin">Rezin</a> of Aram-Damascus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekah">Pekah of Israel</a> set out to depose King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaz">Ahaz</a> of Judah by invasion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> was defeated. According to 2 Chronicles 28 it lost 120,000 troops in a single day, and many of its leading officials were killed, among them the king's son Maaseiah, while many others were carried off as slaves. Telling of the same war, 2 Kings 16:5 says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rezin">Rezin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekah">Pekah</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> but failed to capture it. During the invasion the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a> and Edomites took advantage of Judah's weakness, raiding its towns and villages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Assyrian help came at a price. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaz">Ahaz</a> had to pay tribute to Tiglath-Pileser III from the treasures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> and the royal treasury, and he built idols of Assyrian gods in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> to win favour with his new ally. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> also conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> 7 the prophet tells <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaz">Ahaz</a> that the invasion will fail and urges him to ask God for a sign. When Ahaz refuses, saying he will not test God, Isaiah announces that a virgin shall conceive and bear a son called Immanuel, and that before the child knows how to refuse evil and choose good, the land of the two kings Ahaz fears will be deserted. In Isaiah 8 a second child, Maher-shalal-hash-baz, whose name means Hurry to the spoils, is a sign that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> will plunder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and Ephraim before he can speak. Isaiah ends by calling himself and his children, among them Shear-Jashub, meaning the remnant shall return, signs and symbols in Israel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Syro-Ephraimite War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pekah instead joined Rezin of Damascus in an anti-Assyrian alliance and attacked Judah when its king Ahaz refused to take part, the conflict known as the <b>Syro-Ephraimite War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
